<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    What a client of a broker needs in order to show a deployment to a person, and to seat one.

    A project of its own for the reason Parley.Configuration is one, and in almost the same
    words: two programs read this and cannot otherwise share one reader. The operator's console
    reads it to render a page; `parley terminal` reads it to draw a terminal. Leaving it inside
    Parley.Web would mean a terminal application taking a dependency on an ASP.NET Core assembly
    in order to find out who is in a conversation, which is a boundary nobody would defend if it
    were proposed fresh.

    The split is not arbitrary, and it was measured rather than guessed: of the fifteen types the
    console held outside its Razor pages, exactly one touches HTTP. SeatCookie stays behind; the
    other fourteen are here. Checked by type name rather than by using directive, because
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web supplies implicit global usings and an absent `using` line would have
    proved nothing.

    References Transport for the wire and Configuration for the project manifest, and nothing
    else — which is exactly what Parley.Web referenced before the move, so nothing landed on
    anybody's graph that was not already there. There is no SQLite here and there must never be
    one: SPEC.md section 4 gives the store to one process, and a client that could open it would
    be a second answer to questions the broker already answers. GraphTests enforces that rather
    than trusting this comment.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Parley.Client</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Parley.Client</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Configuration\Parley.Configuration.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Parley.Transport\Parley.Transport.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The one way a client collects and acknowledges an inbox (SPEC.md section 6), shared as source because
       the generated transport holds no hand-written code. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\InboxCollection.cs" Link="InboxCollection.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
